  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "gldpayT3pP1H2M8rr3CEo1KxTRA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:54.082Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Every attachment interaction is three steps \u2014 reserve a slot, send the bytes, later read them back by ref \u2014 and there are two reservation modes. The docs are explicit that they are **equivalent in capability; neither is preferred.** Pick by what the caller can supply.\n\n**Upload-first.** Reserve with just `{ mimeType, fileName }`. The content hash, and therefore the final ref, is known only *after* `send()` resolves. `IAttachmentUpload.ref` is `null` until then.\n\n```typescript\nconst upload = await attachments.reserve({ mimeType: \"image/png\", fileName: \"avatar.png\" });\nconst { ref } = await upload.send(stream);   // ref known here\n```\n\nSimplest when you can stream bytes straight through and do not need the ref until the upload finishes.\n\n**Hash-first.** Hash the content up front and reserve with `{ clientHash, sizeBytes, ... }`. The ref is known at reservation time, which buys two things: you can write it into document state *before or in parallel with* the upload, and dedup can short-circuit before any bytes are sent. The cost is reading the whole file to hash it.\n\n`IAttachmentClient.preprocess(file)` does that hashing, returning `{ ref, hash, sizeBytes, options, data, stream }`. Because it must read the entire file, it is async and buffers in memory \u2014 `file` is a `Blob`, not a stream.\n\n```typescript\nconst results = await attachments.preprocess(file);\n\nawait client.execute(documentId, \"main\", [attachInvoice({ scan: results.ref })]);\nawait attachments.reserve(results.options, (handle) => handle.send(results.stream()));\n```\n\nTwo details that bite. Pass `results.stream()` \u2014 a factory returning a **fresh** `ReadableStream` each call \u2014 rather than the single-use `results.data`, if the upload might be retried. And the execute and the reserve are awaited **separately on purpose**: a failed upload does not roll back the action, and a failed action does not strand an upload. Wrap them in `Promise.all` only if you do not need that separation.\n\nHash-first is also where the verification errors live: `HashMismatch` if uploaded bytes do not match the claimed `clientHash`, `SizeMismatch` if the byte count differs from the declared `sizeBytes`, and `AttachmentAlreadyExists` when the hash is already stored \u2014 which the *client's* `reserve()` catches internally and turns into the existing ref. In React, `useAttachmentUpload()` wraps the whole flow with `status` (`None | Hashing | Uploading | Done | Error`), `progress`, and `error`; progress is stage-based because the remote transport buffers then issues a single PUT.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/09-AttachmentService.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:34:53.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:54.082Z"
    }
  },
